  "prediction": "The report provides an in-depth analysis of the Unemployment Trust Fund (UTF) and the rules governing loans and interest payments for states with funding shortfalls. The UTF is a federal program that provides funds for state unemployment benefits. When a state's unemployment trust fund runs out of money, it can borrow from the U.S. Treasury, with interest.\n\nThe report outlines the conditions states must meet to receive interest-free loans from the UTF. These include repaying the loans by September 30, not making any new loans in the last quarter of the calendar year, meeting funding goals, having a high cost multiple of at least 1.0 in the past five years, and maintaining a certain level of tax effort.\n\nInterest is calculated at the same rate as that paid by the federal government on state reserves in the UTF for the quarter ending December 31 of the previous year, but not higher than 10% per annum. States cannot pay the interest directly or indirectly from SUTA revenue or funds in their state account within the UTF.\n\nThe report also mentions that states can borrow funds without interest from the UTF during the year, but they must meet certain conditions to maintain their interest-free status. Additionally, the report provides a table listing outstanding state loans, accrued interest payments, and whether the state was subject to a credit reduction for tax year 2018. At the time of the report, only the U.S. Virgin Islands had an outstanding loan.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an in-depth analysis of the Unemployment Trust Fund (UTF) and the rules governing loans and interest payments for states with funding shortfalls. The UTF is a federal program that provides funds for state unemployment benefits. When a state's unemployment trust fund runs out of money, it can borrow from the U.S. Treasury, with interest.\n\nThe report outlines the conditions states must meet to receive interest-free loans from the UTF. These include repaying the loans by September 30, not making any new loans in the last quarter of the calendar year, meeting funding goals, having a high cost multiple of at least 1.0 in the past five years, and maintaining a certain level of tax effort.\n\nInterest is calculated at the same rate as that paid by the federal government on state reserves in the UTF for the quarter ending December 31 of the previous year, but not higher than 10% per annum. States cannot pay the interest directly or indirectly from SUTA revenue or funds in their state account within the UTF.\n\nThe report also mentions that states can borrow funds without interest from the UTF during the year, but they must meet certain conditions to maintain their interest-free status. Additionally, the report provides a table listing outstanding state loans, accrued interest payments, and whether the state was subject to a credit reduction for tax year 2018. At the time of the report, only the U.S. Virgin Islands had an outstanding loan.",
  "score": 29.599999514112007,
  "answers": [
    "Although states have a great deal of autonomy in how they establish and run their unemployment insurance programs, federal law requires states to pay Unemployment Compensation (UC) benefits promptly as provided under state law. During some recessions, current taxes and reserve balances may be insufficient to cover state obligations for UC benefits. States may borrow funds from the federal loan account within the Unemployment Trust Fund (UTF) to meet UC benefit obligations. This report summarizes how insolvent states may borrow funds from the UTF loan account to meet their UC benefit obligations. It includes the manner in which states must repay federal UTF loans. It also provides details on how the UTF loans may trigger potential interest accrual and explains the timetable for increased net Federal Unemployment Taxes Act (FUTA) taxes if the funds are not repaid promptly. Outstanding loans listed by state may be found at the Department of Labor's (DOL's) website, https://oui.doleta.gov/unemploy/budget.asp."
  ],
